Sentinel — draft remediation for the nyc_taxi freshness SLA breach (incident 2026-08-01-0314).

## What

The `spark_nyc_taxi_clean` Airflow task has been in `queued` state for 5h 50m, causing the downstream dbt model `nyc_yellow_taxi_trips` to miss its 15-minute freshness SLA. The root cause is worker-pool saturation on the default Airflow pool (`default_pool`).

## Proposed change

1. Move `spark_nyc_taxi_clean` to a dedicated pool (`nyc_taxi_dedicated_pool`) to isolate it from default-pool contention.
2. Add an explicit Airflow SLA of 15 minutes — matches the DataHub freshness assertion (`urn:li:assertion:nyc-taxi-freshness-15m`).
3. Wire an `on_failure_callback` to page the on-call rotation (currently Priya Patel). Sentinel will consume this callback as a future signal source.

## Why

- 4 freshness breaches in the last 7 days on this asset (Sentinel audit, prior 30 days).
- Each breach took >30 minutes to triage manually (per the on-call log).
- The remediation removes the contention root cause and adds a fast feedback loop for Sentinel to consume.

## Risk

- LOW: the dedicated pool already exists (`nyc_taxi_dedicated_pool`, provisioned 2026-06-15).
- LOW: the SLA callback already has a Slack webhook configured (`#sentinel-alerts`).
- MEDIUM: the new SLA tightening (from 30m to 15m in DataHub) requires a separate human approval — Sentinel has proposed it via the DataHub Agent Context Kit; the approval is pending.

